{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/datom.world.md | sed -n '1,70p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t---\n     2\tdescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n     3\t---\n     4\t\n     5\t# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n     6\t\n     7\t## Core Philosophy\n     8\t\n     9\tEverything is data.\n    10\tInterpretation > abstraction.\n    11\tExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\n    12\tRestrictions are a feature.\n    13\t\n    14\t### Foundational Axioms\n    15\t\n    16\t1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n    17\t2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n    18\t3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n    19\t4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n    20\t\n    21\t## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n    22\t\n    23\t- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n    24\t- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n    25\t- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n    26\t- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n    27\t- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n    28\t- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n    29\t\n    30\t## Tuples and Datoms\n    31\t\n    32\tTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\n    33\tA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\n    34\tSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n    35\t\n    36\tSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n    37\t\n    38\t## Streams\n    39\t\n    40\tAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\n    41\tNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\n    42\tSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\n    43\tStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\n    44\tStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n    45\t\n    46\tA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\n    47\tof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\n    48\tsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\n    49\tstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\n    50\tthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\n    51\tanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\n    52\texists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\n    53\tthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\n    54\tand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\n    55\tbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\n    56\tthe stream's own.\n    57\t\n    58\tFunctions can take anything and return anything.\n    59\tAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n    60\t\n    61\tSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\n    62\tSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n    63\t\n    64\t### Host Boundaries\n    65\t\n    66\tA host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\n    67\tsanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\n    68\texhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\n    69\t\n    70\t**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
